So we all can be subject to being deceived. if the targeting is aimed at us and it takes advantage of the way we think and the way and the information we're looking for. So, Dr. Chivri, let me ask you, what are some of the most common cognitive shortcuts or thinking patterns that get us into trouble? I think the first one that we talk about is truth bias. So truth bias is the idea that our default tendency is to think that whatever we hear or read or encounter is true. that people are not always trying to lie to us or deceive us. And that's sort of a precondition for being taken in by any kind of scam or con or fraud.
所以，如果目标针对我们，并且利用了我们的思维方式和我们正在寻找的信息，我们所有人都有可能被欺骗。那么，查布里斯博士，请问，有哪些最常见的认知捷径或思维模式会让我们陷入困境？我认为我们谈到的第一个是“真相偏见”。真相偏见是指我们的默认倾向是认为我们听到、读到或遇到的任何事情都是真实的。人们并不总是试图对我们撒谎或欺骗我们。这是被任何形式的骗局、诈骗或欺诈所欺骗的前提条件。

8
00:04:35.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Because if you believed everything you saw was false or questionable or misleading, then you wouldn't really act on those offers and those promises and those sales pitches and those marketing messages. And truth bias is important for us to have because if we didn't tend to accept other people said we'd never be able to make plans with them, have a conversation with them, or do anything really other than be skeptical all the time. Once you start with truth bias, then other cognitive habits we have can get us into further trouble.
因为如果你认为你所看到的一切都是虚假的、可疑的或误导性的，那么你就不会真正去采取行动回应那些提议、承诺、推销和营销信息。真相偏见对我们很重要，因为如果我们不倾向于接受别人所说的话，我们就永远无法与他们制定计划、进行对话，或者除了时刻保持怀疑之外什么也做不了。一旦你从真相偏见开始，我们拥有的其他认知习惯就会让我们陷入更深的麻烦。

